    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>In a federation a constitution divides power between a central government and member states, each governing its citizens directly in its own fields; neither can abolish the other.</li><li>It differs from a confederation, a league of sovereign states whose common council acts through them, and from a unitary state, whose regions hold only the powers the centre grants.</li><li>Pioneered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1789 and adopted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, federations are now home to about 40 per cent of the world’s people.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> is still officially the Swiss Confederation, Confoederatio Helvetica in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, the source of its abbreviation CH, although it has been a federal state rather than a confederation since its constitution of 1848.</p></aside>
    </section>

